WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.495
AI detection tools estimate whether media may be synthetic or manipulated.

00:00:05.495 --> 00:00:11.430
They can analyze patterns in pixels, frames, audio, compression, or model-specific traces.

00:00:11.430 --> 00:00:17.364
Their output should be treated as one analytical input, not final proof.

00:00:17.364 --> 00:00:22.860
A false positive occurs when genuine or benign media is flagged.

00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:28.355
A false negative occurs when synthetic or manipulated media is missed.

00:00:28.355 --> 00:00:36.487
Consequences can be serious, especially when a result affects reputation, employment, safety, legal action, or public reporting.

00:00:36.487 --> 00:00:41.983
Detection performance changes as generation models, editing pipelines, and platforms change.

00:00:41.983 --> 00:00:47.478
A detector trained on older examples may not recognize new systems.

00:00:47.478 --> 00:00:55.610
Model drift and limited evaluation data can make a published accuracy number misleading for a specific case.

00:00:55.610 --> 00:01:02.864
Platform processing can resize, recompress, filter, or transcode media, altering the signals a detector uses.

00:01:02.864 --> 00:01:10.117
Cropping, screenshots, noise, and adversarial changes can also shift scores without changing the underlying claim.

00:01:10.117 --> 00:01:12.975
Use multiple forms of evidence.

00:01:12.975 --> 00:01:19.349
Review provenance, source history, visual and audio observations, metadata, timeline, and independent corroboration.

00:01:19.349 --> 00:01:27.482
If several detectors agree, ask whether they share training assumptions or failure modes before calling them independent.

00:01:27.482 --> 00:01:32.098
Report the tool, version, input, result, limitations, and interpretation.

00:01:32.098 --> 00:01:35.395
A score is not a verdict.

00:01:35.395 --> 00:01:43.967
Human analysis remains essential for understanding the claim, context, alternative explanations, and potential harm of an incorrect label.
